        "reading_correct": "unsure",
        "reason": "The drawings do not support the measured numbers.\n\nReading. The boundary reading (alpha laths separated by thin bright beta ribs) is the physically right reading for a Ti-6Al-4V BSE image. At 720x, however, many laths are only a few pixels wide and the ribs are barely resolved. The outlines therefore mix two failures:\n- long green and blue regions in the columnar prior-beta grains that clearly span several laths (merged across ribs);\n- many short fragments, especially in the lower basketweave band (laths cut along their length).\n\nThe median aspect ratio of 2.16 is implausibly low for Widmanst\u00e4tten/colony laths; I would expect several times higher. Because laths are both cut and merged, the per-flake numbers are not trustworthy. S = 0.39 is plausible for a multi-colony field, but I would not claim it is within tolerance. Prior-beta boundaries between columns are partly visible in the colour changes, but there are also merges across columns.\n\nMasking.\n- The bottom SEM data bar is filled with flake colours (red/teal at the WD/HV fields), so annotation text and boxes are being measured as flakes.\n- The resin and mount edge at top and bottom are partly outlined.\n- Coverage of 0.645 includes these areas.\n\nScratches. The header reports 0 scratches on the real image. I see no obvious scratch there, so this is fine. The white line in the centre appears to be the mean-direction arrow, not a scratch.\n\nTwin.\n- It is a periodic short-rod array with one straight scratch. It does not resemble the real image (correctly marked untrusted).\n- The method also fails on the twin itself: flake recall 0.19, precision 0.11, aspect-ratio bias -57% (flakes cut short, consistent with the real image), and order-parameter error 0.23.\n- Scratch recall across flakes is 0.15. The header says scratches0 even though a prominent bright scratch crosses the whole twin image and flakes are drawn across it.\n- Worst family tolerance ratio is 7.5x.\n\nHistory. The same set (distance 0) was already run with this reading and failed with essentially the same holdout score (7.48). The current tuning has not moved anything.\n\nConfidence: high that the result should be rejected; moderate on the exact failure mix in the real image, given the low resolution.",
        "next_step": "1) Fix masking before any tuning:\n- exclude the SEM info bar (crop the bottom ~12% of rows, or add a data-bar detector: uniform dark band with text boxes);\n- add a specimen mask that drops the near-black resin above and below the sample (threshold on local mean intensity, e.g. keep pixels above about 35% of the sample median), with a 5\u201310 px erosion at the sample edge.\n\n2) Address resolution. Laths are about 2\u20134 px wide, so upsample the real image 2x before ridge detection. Halve the boundary ridge scale accordingly (ridge sigma about 1.5 px -> 0.75 px at native size, or keep the sigma and upsample). Lower the minimum ridge contrast so the faint beta ribs separate the long merged laths. Raise the gap-closing length along the lath direction, and stop splitting on short gaps or across the lath (oriented closing aligned with the local orientation field), to stop fragmentation. Watch whether the median aspect ratio rises from about 2 towards a believable value; check this only on the real drawings plus the rotation test.\n\n3) Scratch detector. It misses a full-width bright straight line on the twin (recall 0.15). Lower the scratch minimum line contrast, lower the required straightness or min length so that a line spanning about 70% of the image width qualifies, and make sure it runs before flake segmentation so flakes are not merged or cut across it.\n\n4) Replace the twin generator for this set: long, thin alpha laths in colonies and basketweave, inside columnar prior-beta grains, with thin bright beta ribs, BSE-like noise, and lath widths of 2\u20134 px. Until a trusted twin exists, the tolerance claims cannot be checked.\n\nIf, after the upsampling and masking changes, laths are still merged across ribs, then no parameter of the boundary reading can resolve laths at this magnification. The honest outcome is to report only image-level direction and S, from a structure-tensor orientation field, and drop per-flake aspect ratio and misalignment for this set."
